\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Time reflection on a spatial momentum axis}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The \vocabk{spinor time map}{fdip-017Z} takes the ordinary
\vocabk{translation block}{fdip-016C} at #{r} onto the one at
#{-\theta_s r}, where #{\theta_s} negates the time component
of a label. For a non-self-opposite label #{r} supported on
a spatial axis #{c}, take distinct spatial transverse axes
#{a,b} of equal period, both different from #{t,c}. Thus
#{n\ge4}. Use the lifted quarter-turn #{u_{ab}} of
\ref{fdip-016X} at #{r} and its inverse at #{-r}, with the
same primitive eighth root. The time map preserves the
exponent in this \vocabk{cyclic grading}{fdip-016I}.}

\proof{
\p{Choose primitive roots for the coordinate periods and write
the character as #{\alpha_r(x)=\prod_j\zeta_j^{r_jx_j}}.
A field in the block is #{\psi(x)=\alpha_r(x)v}: set #{v}
equal to its value at zero in the translation identity.
The value action of #{S_t} conjugates the unit-modulus scalar
#{\alpha_r(\theta_s x)}. The new character is therefore
#{\alpha_{-\theta_s r}}. Its constant value ranges over all
of #{S\otimes F} by bijectivity, giving the entire target
block. On the spatial axis #{\theta_s r=r}.}

\p{The spin action fixes each spatial direction under
conjugation, as in \ref{fdip-0182}. The factor
#{u_{ab}=(\bbI_S+\varepsilon_a\varepsilon_b)/\sqrt{2}}
has real coefficients and is fixed as well. Its coordinate
quarter-turn commutes with the time reflection since their
directions are disjoint. Hence #{S_t} also commutes with
the full action of the turn by spin and coordinate pullback
in \ref{fdip-016Y}. If the source multiplier for that turn
is #{\zeta^\nu}, conjugate linearity gives #{\zeta^{-\nu}}
for the same turn on the image. The chosen target generator
is its inverse, giving #{\zeta^\nu} and the same exponent.
This supplies the time action in \ref{fdip-0177}.}

\p{These are ordinary Fourier blocks of the field space.
For a coupled operator to restrict to them, it must commute
with translations. The turn must also act on the coupled
data for its exponents to label that symmetry. Trivial links
meet both requirements. The argument of \ref{fdip-016L} gives the
corresponding checks on transformed backgrounds.}
}
